Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 17:59:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09477/11362] Correct the AVO entry: the ARC result is not the
 evolutionary loop

The entry committed hours ago in 4d66a16c4d described AVO as agent-as-mutation-
operator inside an evolutionary search, then said it also scored 100% on the
ARC-AGI-3 public set. Those are two different results and the entry read as
though one mechanism produced both. Correcting it, because the planner reads
this file as required input and would have planned against the wrong mechanism.

Established from primary sources this time. arXiv 2603.24517 is "AVO: Agentic
Variation Operators for Autonomous Evolutionary Search"; its abstract covers
attention-kernel optimization on Blackwell only and does not mention ARC-AGI-3
at all -- verified by fetching the paper. The evolutionary operator, the seven
days, the 500+ directions and the 40 committed kernel versions are the KERNEL
work. For ARC, NVIDIA's blog says the agent built no explicit world models and
acted directly on text-only 64x64 grids, and credits persistent memory,
supervision, and long-horizon scaffolding around a frontier reasoner. Public
games only, no component ablations, no published cost.

The consequence is not cosmetic. Copying the evolutionary operator because of
the ARC score copies the wrong mechanism. And the half of the directive that
survives is the harder one: what NVIDIA credits for ARC is a frontier reasoner,
which is exactly what the scored path may not use. Supervision and persistent
memory transfer; open-ended strategy generation does not.

Appended rather than rewritten, per never-prune. The original paragraph stays as
written because it is what the operator directive was issued against, and a
reader needs to see what was believed at the time.

